===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} [[dreadfully|Dreadfully]].
#* {{quote-book|en |year=2003|title=Of Preachers and Pagans |author=David Davis |page=199|ISBN=|passage=I'm sorry, Miz Terrigan. I'm '''dreadful''' sorry.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=2007 |title=Professor At The Breakfast Table |author=Oliver Wendell Holmes |page=130|ISBN=|passage=You don't look so '''dreadful''' poor in the face as you did a while back.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=2015 |title=Jessica's first prayer: A Christian Fiction of Hesba Stretton|author=Hesba Stretton |page=|ISBN=|passage="No," she replied, coolly, "and I shall want my dinner '''dreadful''' bad afore I get it, I know. You don't often feel '''dreadful''' hungry, do you, sir?}}

====Usage notes====
The senses of "dreadful" synonymous with "[[afraid]]" similarly use the infinitive or the preposition "of": ''they were dreadful to build'' or ''the boy was dreadful of his majesty''. These senses are, however, now obsolete.

When used as an intensifier, "dreadful" is actually a form of the adverb "[[dreadfully]]" and thus considered informal or vulgar.

